niques that i was working  with in my job as a particle physicist  could possibly apply to my art making  and help me to generate fresh new  movements that were nevertheless in my  own style  so i wasn't sure exactly how this would  turn out  i had a couple of goals as i got started  i wanted the generated movements to look  realistic you know like a like a human  being on earth preferably uh with forces  of friction and gravity present  i also wanted it to authentically look  like my own style what you know whatever  that meant to me i wanted it to be  movements that i probably would do in a  studio  and more than anything i was hoping for  movements that would surprise me and  inspire me to get up and moving which is  this final point obviously which is  kinetic desire that i just wanted  something that would give me that desire  to get up in a dance studio and move  so i should mention that the use of  technology and dance has a long and  interesting and rich history  in it's been spanning decades in uh the  history of modern post-modern and  contemporary dance so many prominent  choreographers in this discipline have  used algorithms equations games of  chance motion capture even and more to  generate work over the years  so in some ways what i'm presenting  today is nothing new  in other ways i would say though the  incorporation of neural networks in  particular and choreography is a  relatively recent phenomenon and it  feels very cool to be one of the people  who has been interested in pushing that  specific intersection forward over the  past few years  so when i started this project in 2017  there was some previous work in applying  neural networks to choreography so this  gives you a sense of the state of the  art at the time  so on the left is a project called core  rnn and on the right is a project that  was a collaboration between google arts  and culture and the choreographer wayne  mcgregor  so  both projects  kind of have a similar model for the  human body  with around 15 to 20 points representing  their motion  and both of them i would say had  some difficulties capturing full  three-dimensionality of a dancer's  movements the  project on the left used an xbox kinect  camera to detect a pose which is a  single camera facing front so if your  arm is occluded from the camera it's  difficult for the software to figure out  where your body is  and on the right uh they actually used  video archives of mcgregor's work and  extracted poses from those  two-dimensional video frames so it  suffered kind of the same difficulty  both of them also used sequential models  in machine learning that were designed  for time series predictions  so we can kind of contrast those with  the  the type of data that i was able to  extract using motion capture studio at  my phd institution that  used a multi-camera setup of 20  different  motion capture cameras so you can see  that it's really able to capture all the  kinds of rotational motion that i might  come up with in a studio  and i was also able to capture around 50  points on my body representing different  areas  and this multi-camera setup allowed for  nearly  gapless data taking so i could even  roll on the floor and it was able to  interpolate where some of the points on  my body needed to be at those points  so here's how we we collected that data  it's a it's the classic motion capture  suit with a bunch of ping pong balls  that are reflective all over my body um  so they're collecting xyz uh components  for each of the points on my body at  around 35 uh frames per second  so it's a pretty um high dimensional  data set um by some metrics and uh  so what i'll share with you today are  two different projects that i led with  different independent research groups  and uh together we kind of pioneered the  application of a couple different  architectures and machine learning to  motion capture of choreography so the  first project i'll talk about is a  variational autoencoder which is a vae  for short and then the second project is  a graph neural network or a gnn  um yeah and sorry i should say on the  bottom is the multi-camera setup where  you can see all the different camera  angles that were  viewing my movements  great so this first project is the  variational auto encoder and my research  team was composed of many different  types of experts  some  physicists who i collaborated with in my  particle physics work uh others who were  interested in the digital humanities and  also some  dance choreographers and researchers  and we worked together for a couple  years and then published our work in the  summer of 2019.  so the architecture that we designed is  a variational autoencoder  and an autoencoder is a kind of machine  learning architecture that tries to  generate an output that closely  replicates the inputs and the catch is  that often in these models there's an  information bottleneck that's built into  the architecture so here you can kind of  see this classic bow tie shape where the  innermost layer of the model is highly  reduced compared to the size of your  inputs and your outputs  and so after quite a lot of iteration  and training eventually the model should  find  an efficient  and lower dimensional representation of  your input in that latent dimension  and  moreover the model that we built is a  variational autoencoder which sort of  extends that basic autoencoder structure  to allow for sampling and for generation  of new material  so  rather than learning a specific point to  represent every input sequence that i  showed the model the model learns a  probability distribution for each of the  latent dimensions present in the system  and then i sample from those latent  probability distributions to then  generate new material  so here are some examples of randomly  sampling from the latent space of this  model so each of these movements are  fully generated  and you can see that i think in my view  they check the box of realism they  mostly look like humans existing on  earth there's some kind of interesting  artifacts and shaking sometimes but  i think for the most part it was able to  capture a lot of really nice  diverse and interesting movements even  though these might not be  necessarily the most exciting movements  that you could imagine doing in a dance  studio so i would say these are typical  movements of the latent space  but  what i was excited to try is to try  sampling from parts of the latent space  that were kind of on the fringes of the  actual manifold of my real data so sort  of the areas that had less coverage in  real data and see what some of the more  unusual movements that i could construct  would look like  so that process is exactly what i did to  construct the following video piece  which is also made up fully of  generative movements  [Music]  [Music]  all right i can't show the whole thing  unfortunately  so uh so that was an example of sort of  just working with the generated  animations of the outputs um i'm also  interested in live performance kind of  first and foremost so i've had a couple  um  experiences of trying to put those  generated movements back onto a human  body  so this was from a very early work in  progress of  having myself and a couple other dancers  respond live on stage to some generated  paths  generated by the model  and then also my collaborator raymond  who's a phenomenal dancer  did a performance in the motion capture  studio itself where our data was  collected  and that allowed him to get live  feedback from the model in the following  way  so projected on the screen  there are a couple different um  segments of what the model is responding  to so you can see his actual  motion capture points in the middle on  the right is kind of where along uh we  call it like the phi rotational axis so  it's rotation around z which angle he's  facing  and on the left is maybe the most  interesting which is where in the latent  space is his movement at any one time  and so as a dancer his prompt was to  play with staying in one area of the  latent space and trying to figure out  what movements belonged in that area  versus traversing far across the laden  space and trying to challenge the model  in that way  [Music]  so that was really fun um and that could  have been maybe the end of the story was  generating new material with this model  but we also found  another interesting way to put it to  good use so we found that beyond just  generating outputs this model was also  pretty good at creating variations on  existing phrases that i might find  interesting as a choreographer but might  want to tweak in some non-trivial way  so uh this is kind of a cartoon of what  this looks like but because the model  clusters related phrases together in the  latent space you can explore neighboring  sequences in this latent material and  then see how they uh how they align so  for example the top two phrases that  i've generated here um  both seem to share that they are on the  floor whereas these two movements on the  bottom kind of share this timing of  moving from uh crouch to standing so  there's kind of these interesting higher  dimensional relationships that are  shared between  these different sequences  so we can exploit this fact uh in a  really interesting way  so we can  look at the specific latent embedding of  a given sequence so in this case the  black sequence here represents it is my  actual motion capture data that we use  to train the model  whereas in blue we see the model's  generated  output representing this phrase  and you can see that the model's pretty  well trained because it's captured a lot  of the relevant kinematics that make  this sequence kind of unique  but now if i take this latent embedding  that the model has to represent this  input phrase and then i add a little bit  of noise to that original point so i  kind of deviate from the original point  and decode a new phrase that is nearby  then the result is that i get a new  movement that's very closely related to  the original sequence but has a  modification in this case it's at the  end of the sequence you can see that the  movement rotates a little bit  and you can imagine playing with this  quite a bit so  if you deviate even further  you get a movement that still is related  to the original kind of choreographic  intent of that phrase but deviates  even further  so it's a fascinating exercise to trace  how a single choreographic idea  you know translates into something else  as you move further and further from  that original point  it was using this procedure that around  last spring  at the onset of the pandemic i was able  to generate the following video  which is a duet with myself  so since i knew that i wasn't going to  be able to dance in person with someone  for a long time  it was something i was interested in  doing early on  at the time i thought that was going to  be maybe a few weeks and it ended up  being a bit longer than that  so next i'll show you an excerpt from a  video of this duet with myself  [Music]  [Applause]  so um so that sort of concludes the  first um you know arch of this effort  and uh the story continues a little bit  which is that uh this past summer i was  an intern in intel's ai group and i was  able to extend this work with a new set  of collaborators and uh  they had expertise ranging from  reinforcement learning to graph neural  networks uh to generic deep learning  so the motivations for this study were a  little different so rather than just  focusing on generating new material in  the best way that we could i was  interested in turning the analysis  inward and understanding relationships  between different parts of my body while  i danced and this was exciting to me  because this is actually what i think  about when i dance is how does my head  relate to my feet you know how is how  are my muscles reacting to certain  inputs so i was curious to see what a  machine learning model might tell me  about some of those important  relationships and whether they would  necessarily align with the way that i  actually feel like my body interacts  with itself as i move  so technically what that ended up  resulting in is modifying the latent  embedding of each given sequence to  include an explicit graph structure  representing each relationship between  parts of my body while i moved so  essentially it was a graph based  variational autoencoder  and graph neural networks as a theme are  neural networks designed to operate on  graph structures where a graph here is a  collection of nodes and edges where  edges represent relationships between  those nodes  so then in the neural network structure  those features associated with each of  the nodes and edges are then updated  periodically by aggregating information  from local subgraphs or neighborhoods  near those points  the structure of the model was based on  this project called nri or neural  relational inference which is a project  by thomas kipp max welling and quite a  few others  and the idea of this is to learn a  latent interaction graph to describe a  dynamical system of interacting  particles where you might not  necessarily know all of the  interactions that are happening  so in in this case for example in this  sort of toy model of a couple particles  that are orbiting each other  you have each pairwise particle  relationship assigned as one of some  number of edge types where edge types  kind of means what's the quality of the  interaction between these two particles  and that can include no edge meaning  that there's no relationship between  those types  so  excuse me our model as in nri  includes a graph neural network based  encoder and decoder and the encoder's  job is basically to output probability  distributions for each of the edges that  are present so i i input the same raw  data that i used to train the  variational auto encoder that i 