kes sense to me that my right hand  would be pretty articulate and then also  the way that my hand moves affects the  opposite leg in my body and then there's  also edge type 1 and edge type 3 that  also describe  relationships that all originate from  kind of one localized area of my body  and then those have ripple effects that  dictate movements in a very distant part  of my body so edge type one all of these  edges originate in my left hand and then  connect to my upper torso  whereas edge type three these edges  originate in my left foot and then  extend up to both of my hands  so i found it really interesting that  the model was picking up on these  relationships that i might not have been  able to write down based on my dance  training but that feel very  authentically true to the way that i  move  so we published this work uh last winter  in the nurbs workshop on machine  learning for creativity and design  i wish i had videos to show you of what  performances with this work looks like  but i haven't had access to dancers or a  dance studio for a number of years  because of this pandemic  so instead i did what any creator might  do in this instance which was make a  tick tock account and  i was locked in my house basically so i  was writing my thesis and then as i was  taking breaks on writing my thesis i  would get up and try to improvise using  prompts from this model  so i have a couple clips of what that  looked like  so on the left i have a prompt from a  highly ranked connection from the model  and in this case that's a connection  from the top of my head to my wrist my  right wrist  uh and so according to the model this  was a really important uh relationship  to think about in generating my future  movements and i found that as an  improvisational prompt it was pretty  useful it was um  yeah playful generative i feel like i  was having a lot of fun and thinking of  movements that felt good  then on the right i tried a really lowly  ranked connection 