s are so  awesome and appealing  i did not expect to be so  [Laughter]  i did not expect to be so delighted by  under-trained models or you know weird  failure modes  so they can be hilarious  um i've also found that they can just be  beautiful  you know ways that it can create these  imageries that i could imagine trying to  code for hours and hours  so they've been totally surprising in  that aspect also  and then  lastly um it's also taught me a couple  different ways of conceiving of my  relationship with a machine learning  model in a creative context  and that kind of relationship was  constantly shifting and so that's kind  of what i wanted to leave you guys with  today  so this is just my personal like mental  map um of how i've started to think  about this relationship so here the  y-axis kind of represents who has the  creative power or the autonomy in this  in this system is the model making those  creative choices or am i making those  creative choices  and then along the x-axis i have is the  model your friend or is the model not  your friend uh whatever that might mean  and i i feel like this model this  project has spent some time in kind of  each of these quadrants throughout its  evolution  it's ranged from you know automating in  the sense of just generating new  material and that's kind of the final  result  to uh generating like a mountain of  sequences and then i can kind of cherry  pick which phrases seem interesting to  me and then i craft a video piece out of  those outputs  um  it's also served as kind of like an  external eye critiquing my practice and  showing me what it sees in the way that  i move  sort of like an alien looking at my  movements  and then at times it has felt like it's  having its own creative experience it's  responding to my movements and it's  creating a relationship between my  previous movements and the current  existence of that model  so  all of all of these i think in the  future i'm kind of most excited about  th