sented like were actually movements  isolated to one arm that were like local  to each other  in the graph representation if that  makes sense yeah  there are a couple instances where it  really fixated on feet um like one edge  type was just a single foot and all of  the connections within that foot and i  think it kind of depended on  what part of the movement data set it  was looking at and where the  articulation was going on  i did expect it to to pick up more on  skeletal structure i thought maybe you  would only find like skeletal  relationships  that was one of the inputs to the graph  model is each edge was labeled with a  zero or one based on whether or not it  belonged to what i described as the  skeleton so it had a little bit of that  information already um  but yeah for the most part i felt like  it picked up on these like crossbody and  only a minority of the edge types were  really located in one single area  thanks  first up great talk  you mentioned the use of this  research for preservation right  any words on what others other dancers  are not familiar with machine learning  or  data  representation in general i mean any any  thoughts on how this can be made more  general purpose for  non-engineers  yeah it's an interesting question  because when i when i talk about this  work to  technology or science people  people seem to be really interested in  it when i talk with choreographers and  dancers by and large people are kind of  like  why  we have existing practices in the dance  world to kind of you know  preserve someone's legacy and someone's  ideas and  those can't be captured in a machine  learning model like this they're  captured in language and in  relationships and  when i've learned pieces that are from  choreographers it's often taught by  someone who has already performed that  piece or someone who trained in their  company and there's kind of this oral  history that's really valued in the  field so i think it's personal it it  works 