rious  when we're looking at that that like  two-dimensional representation of where  they currently were in the latent space  is that just a function of  their current position as in you know  regardless of what they've done five  seconds ago if they were in the same  pose now they'd be in that same spot in  the latent space now or is the  distribution over something more like a  stochastic process wherein yeah  the exact thing he did 10 seconds ago  would have affected his position now  even if you were in the same position  now in two different scenarios yeah  totally um  so the performance with raymond i'm  trying to remember it may actually have  been  a pose space like a space of poses  rather than sequences  in which case it was responding to you  know at a given frame of time  what does that correspond to in the  model and so that might have had better  inference um for the purposes of his  work where maybe waiting five seconds to  uh to get each movement sequence and  then getting input from the model  wouldn't give him enough time so we  played around with both models we had  kind of the sequence based model which  is what i used for most of this talk but  we also have a model of just poses and  so you can imagine tracing any line  through the latent space there and then  constructing a sequence of those poses  um but yeah we we loaded the trained  model into like a tensorflow.js system  and we were able to kind of ping that in  real time with the motion capture suit  so in the video that we're looking at um  it looks like you'd or it's generated  based off of like you had planted one  foot and then pivoted on the ball of the  foot but based on the way that the space  is recorded  it looks like it's always  centered at zero zero  do you think that um the lack of  absolute positioning  kind of confused it in that it sort of  thought hey it's fine mariel can just  arbitrarily start spinning in place  um like do you do you think it could  become more robust w